{"entries":{"chapter-83-study-kidney-diseases|Chapter 83 Study: Kidney Diseases":["u1"],"summary|Summary":["u2"],"ckd-definition-amp-diagnosis|CKD — Definition & Diagnosis":["u3"],"copy|CKD criteria: eGFR <60 mL/min/1.73 m² (normal young-adult GFR ~125; <15 = kidney failure), albuminuria (ACR >30 mg/g), hematuria (any degree), urinary casts, or imaging abnormality — any persisting >3 months (p1278).":["u4"],"copy|CKD is usually asymptomatic until GFR falls to roughly 15–20 mL/min/1.73 m² (p1278).":["u5"],"copy|US CKD prevalence ~11.5% overall (4.8% stages 1–2, 6.7% stages 3–5) but 47% in adults >70y (p1278).":["u6"],"ckd-management|CKD — Management":["u7"],"copy|BP target in CKD: SBP 120–130, DBP <90 (p1279); goal of <130/80 for diabetes + CKD (p1286).":["u8"],"copy|ACEI/ARB (RAS blockade) is the mainstay in diabetes when DN is suspected; do not combine ACEI+ARB (↑ complications) (p1279).":["u9"],"copy|SGLT2 inhibitors and GLP-1 receptor agonists reduce CKD-progression risk in diabetics; benefit in nondiabetics is still emerging (p1279–1280).":["u10"],"copy|Statins (± ezetimibe) recommended once eGFR <60 (p1280).":["u11"],"copy|Low-protein diet (0.5–0.6 g/kg/d) or very-low (0.3–0.4 g/kg/d) — some evidence it may reduce progression to ESKD in CKD stage 4–5; in nondiabetic CKD stage ≤3 it does not appear to reduce progression (p1280).":["u12"],"copy|Discuss dialysis preferences once eGFR falls <~20 (p1280).":["u13"],"copy|Nephrology referral triggers (KCP list, p1278): AKI, red cell casts, refractory HTN, K⁺ abnormalities, recurrent/extensive stones, hereditary kidney disease, CKD stage 4–5, severely increased albuminuria. K/DIGO's own restated list (p1290) phrases the GFR trigger as GFR <30 instead.":["u14"],"aki|AKI":["u15"],"copy|Classified by urine output: nonoliguric >400 mL/day, oliguric 100–400, anuric <100 — nonoliguric has the better prognosis (p1280).":["u16"],"copy|Categorized prerenal / intrarenal / postrenal (Table 83-1, p1281–1282).":["u17"],"copy|NSAID use doubles AKI risk in adults >65 (p1282).":["u18"],"copy|CI-AKI (contrast-induced) risk factors: old age, CKD, diabetes, heart failure, volume depletion, other nephrotoxins (p1282).":["u19"],"glomerulonephritis-rpgn|Glomerulonephritis / RPGN":["u20"],"copy|IgA nephropathy is the most common primary GN worldwide; 30–40% progress to ESKD within 20 years; ESKD incidence is almost 2× higher in patients older than 50 (p1283).":["u21"],"copy|Postinfectious GN (PIGN): hematuria + proteinuria 10–14 days post-infection; hypocomplementemia in >70%; ~50% need acute dialysis; only ~20% achieve complete recovery, ~30% progress to ESKD (p1283).":["u22"],"copy|RPGN classification — Table 83-2 (p1284).":["u23"],"copy|Corticosteroids are the mainstay; cyclophosphamide for crescentic/proliferative disease or life-threatening complications (p1284–1285); rituximab is roughly equivalent potency to cyclophosphamide (p1285).":["u24"],"nephrotic-syndrome|Nephrotic Syndrome":["u25"],"copy|Definition: proteinuria >3 g/day + hypoalbuminemia + edema + dyslipidemia (p1285).":["u26"],"copy|Most common biopsy findings in older adults with NS: membranous nephropathy (MN) + minimal change disease (MCD) + amyloidosis = ~60% combined — MCD ~15%, MN ~30–40% of biopsies (p1285).":["u27"],"copy|Amyloidosis found on ~10% of biopsies done for presumed idiopathic NS (p1285).":["u28"],"copy|Treatment: RAS blockade for proteinuria; sodium restriction <2 g/day + loop diuretics for edema (p1286).":["u29"],"diabetic-nephropathy-dn|Diabetic Nephropathy (DN)":["u30"],"copy|~30% of diabetics develop DN in their lifetime; DN is the leading cause of NS and dialysis-ESKD in the US (p1286).":["u31"],"copy|Accelerated kidney-function loss once eGFR falls <45 mL/min/1.73 m² (p1286).":["u32"],"copy|ACEI/ARB, SGLT2i, and GLP-1 RA all delay CKD→ESKD progression; BP goal <130/80 (p1286).":["u33"],"acute-interstitial-nephritis-ain|Acute Interstitial Nephritis (AIN)":["u34"],"copy|~80% of older patients show partial/complete recovery within 6 months (p1287).":["u35"],"copy|Most cases in older adults are drug-induced (antibiotics, PPIs, NSAIDs, diuretics); stopping the offending drug is the key intervention (p1287).":["u36"],"copy|Corticosteroids improve outcomes if started within 14 days of first symptoms (p1287, main text).":["u37"],"copy|Rare: airway involvement can cause bronchospasm (p1287).":["u38"],"renovascular-disease|Renovascular Disease":["u39"],"copy|Prevalence 2–3% of general hypertensive population; up to 40% of refractory hypertension (p1287).":["u40"],"copy|Atherosclerosis accounts for almost 90% of renovascular cases in older adults (p1287), and a recognized nonatherosclerotic cause is fibromuscular dysplasia; Hazzard prints “fibrous dysplasia” in the paragraph and in Table 83-3 (p1288).":["u41"],"copy|Duplex ultrasound sensitivity/specificity: low-to-mid 90% range (p1288).":["u42"],"copy|Renal vein renin ratio ≥1.5 between kidneys predicts a beneficial BP response to revascularization; failure to lateralize does not predict a negative response; overall BP response cannot be predicted confidently with renal vein renin measurements (p1289).":["u43"],"copy|CORAL trial (947 patients): no benefit of revascularization vs. medical management (p1289).":["u44"],"copy|Up to 50% of stenoses worsen over time, at up to 5%/year (p1289).":["u45"],"eskd-epidemiology-amp-referral|ESKD — Epidemiology & Referral":["u46"],"copy|ESKD/kidney failure = GFR <15 mL/min/1.73 m² (p1289).":["u47"],"copy|~10–13% of adults in North America, Europe, and Asia are estimated to have some form of CKD (p1290).":["u48"],"copy|Historic untreated CKD decline rate ~7–10 mL/min/year (p1290).":["u49"],"copy|~60% of US ESKD patients have nausea/vomiting at the time dialysis is initiated (p1291).":["u50"],"renal-replacement-therapy-choices|Renal Replacement Therapy Choices":["u51"],"copy|Conventional hemodialysis: 3×/week, ~4 h/session; access options are AV fistula (best outcomes), AV graft, or temporary catheter (high bacteremia rates) (p1291–1292).":["u52"],"copy|AV fistula matures over ~3 months before it's usable (p1292).":["u53"],"copy|Peritoneal dialysis: continuous cyclic or nocturnal PD preferred in long-term care (p1293).":["u54"],"copy|Among patients >75 placed on a kidney-transplant waitlist, <40% receive a transplant within 5 years (p1293).":["u55"],"copy|A time-limited dialysis trial is typically predetermined at 4–6 weeks (p1293).":["u56"],"eskd-complications|ESKD Complications":["u57"],"copy|Anemia: typically develops around GFR ~35 mL/min; normochromic, normocytic (p1294). The chapter gives a conservative hemoglobin level near 11.5 g/dL and misprints the unit as mg/dL (p1294); KDIGO 2026, recommendation 3.3.1 advises an ESA treatment target below 11.5 g/dL in adults with CKD.":["u58"],"copy|Hazzard describes ferritin <500 ng/mL or transferrin saturation <30% under its historical “functional iron deficiency” wording (p1294). In anemic patients receiving hemodialysis, KDIGO 2026 recommendation 2.1 suggests starting iron when both ferritin is ≤500 ng/mL and transferrin saturation is ≤30%.":["u59"],"copy|Cardiovascular death risk on dialysis: 10–100× the general-population risk (p1294).":["u60"],"copy|Secondary hyperparathyroidism: PTH begins rising by GFR ~40 mL/min (p1295).":["u61"],"copy|Pyridoxine (vitamin B6) supplementation for malnutrition: 50 mg/day (p1295).":["u62"],"copy|Pruritus affects up to 40% of ESKD patients; low-dose gabapentin reduces uremic pruritus; avoid diphenhydramine/hydroxyzine (anticholinergic burden) (p1296).":["u63"],"prognosis-amp-end-of-life|Prognosis & End of Life":["u64"],"copy|1-year survival for octogenarians/nonagenarians after starting dialysis: 50% per the chapter's own Key Clinical Points (p1278) — see book contradiction below.":["u65"],"copy|87% of nursing-home residents died or had decreased functional status within 1 year of starting hemodialysis (p1296).":["u66"],"copy|Hospice use is 5-fold lower among hemodialysis patients than among older non-dialysis patients (p1297).":["u67"],"copy|The \"surprise question\" (would you be surprised if this patient died in 6 months?), combined with albumin, age, dementia, and peripheral vascular disease, is a validated mortality predictor (p1296).":["u68"],"drugs-name-dose-target-key-cautions|Drugs (name — dose/target — key cautions)":["u69"],"copy|Phosphate binders (calcium acetate, sevelamer, lanthanum carbonate) — for hyperphosphatemia not controlled by diet alone (p1280).":["u70"],"copy|Calcitriol (active vitamin D analog) — for PTH unresponsive to plain vitamin D supplementation; caution: hypercalcemia and hyperphosphatemia (p1280).":["u71"],"copy|Pyridoxine — 50 mg/day for malnutrition (p1295); gabapentin — low-dose, for uremic pruritus (p1296); avoid diphenhydramine/hydroxyzine in ESKD pruritus (anticholinergic burden) (p1296).":["u72"],"book-contradiction-exam-trap|Book contradiction (exam trap)":["u73"],"copy|1-year dialysis survival for octogenarians/nonagenarians is given as two different numbers in the same chapter: the opening Key Clinical Points box says 50% (p1278), but the \"Prognosis and Survival\" section later states 54% (p1296) for the identical statistic. Both are printed as-is; know which figure a question is quoting.":["u74"],"recall|Recall":["u75"],"copy|CHAPTER 83s / HAZZARD":["u76"],"copy|Study summary of Chapter 83; the book and the full chapter are the reference.":["u77"],"copy|1.":["u78"],"copy|What eGFR, albuminuria, and duration criteria define CKD?":["u79"],"copy|eGFR <60 mL/min/1.73 m² (or albuminuria >30 mg/g, hematuria, urinary casts, or imaging abnormality), persisting more than 3 months (p1278).":["u80"],"copy|3.":["u81"],"copy|What blood pressure target is recommended for older adults with CKD?":["u82"],"copy|Systolic 120–130 mm Hg, diastolic under 90 mm Hg (p1279).":["u83"],"copy|4.":["u84"],"copy|At what eGFR does statin therapy become specifically recommended in CKD, and is combining ACEI with ARB advised?":["u85"],"copy|eGFR under 60 mL/min/1.73 m².":["u86"],"copy|Combining ACEI and ARB is generally not advised — it increases complications (p1279–1280).":["u87"],"copy|5.":["u88"],"copy|How is AKI classified by urine output, and which category has the best prognosis?":["u89"],"copy|Nonoliguric (>400 mL/day), oliguric (100–400 mL/day), anuric (<100 mL/day); nonoliguric AKI has the better prognosis (p1280).":["u90"],"copy|7.":["u91"],"copy|What proportion of IgA nephropathy patients progress to ESKD within 20 years, and how does age at onset affect ESKD risk?":["u92"],"copy|30–40% progress to ESKD within 20 years; ESKD incidence is almost twice as high in patients older than 50 (p1283).":["u93"],"copy|8.":["u94"],"copy|What three diagnoses account for about 60% of kidney-biopsy findings in older adults with nephrotic syndrome?":["u95"],"copy|Membranous nephropathy, minimal change disease, and amyloidosis (p1285).":["u96"],"copy|9.":["u97"],"copy|At what eGFR does diabetic nephropathy show accelerated loss of kidney function, and what is the BP goal in diabetes plus CKD?":["u98"],"copy|eGFR below 45 mL/min/1.73 m²; BP goal under 130/80 mm Hg (p1286).":["u99"],"copy|10.":["u100"],"copy|What proportion of older patients with acute interstitial nephritis recover partially or completely within 6 months, and what is the single most important management step?":["u101"],"copy|About 80%; the most important step is stopping the offending drug (p1287).":["u102"],"copy|11.":["u103"],"copy|What proportion of renovascular disease in older adults is due to atherosclerosis, and what did the CORAL trial show about revascularization?":["u104"],"copy|About 90%; CORAL (947 patients) showed no benefit of revascularization over medical management (p1287, p1289).":["u105"],"copy|13.":["u106"],"copy|Around what GFR does renal anemia typically develop, and what type of anemia is it?":["u107"],"copy|Around GFR 35 mL/min; a normochromic, normocytic anemia due to inadequate erythropoietin production (p1294).":["u108"],"copy|15.":["u109"],"copy|The chapter states two different 1-year survival rates for octogenarians/nonagenarians starting dialysis — what are they, and where does each appear?":["u110"],"copy|50% (Key Clinical Points, p1278) and 54% (Prognosis and Survival section, p1296) — the same statistic printed differently in two places in the same chapter.":["u111"],"copy|Answer":["u112","u113","u114","u115","u116","u117","u118","u119","u120","u121","u122"],"copy|Atherosclerosis accounts for almost 90% of renovascular disease in the geriatric population. Hazzard calls the other cause “fibrous dysplasia” in the prose (p1287) and Table 83-3 (p1288).Current practice (not the book): the accepted vascular diagnosis is fibromuscular dysplasia — International consensus, 2019.":["u41"],"copy|Anemia in CKD is typically normochromic and normocytic and develops around GFR 35 mL/min. Hazzard prints a conservative hemoglobin target “near 11.5 mg/dL” (p1294). [sic — hemoglobin is measured in g/dL here.]Current practice (not the book): in adults receiving an ESA, target hemoglobin below 11.5 g/dL — KDIGO 2026, recommendation 3.3.1.":["u270"],"copy|Hazzard describes ferritin <500 ng/mL or transferrin saturation <30% under its historical “functional iron deficiency” wording (p1294).Current practice (not the book): for anemic patients receiving hemodialysis, KDIGO 2026 recommendation 2.1 suggests starting iron when both ferritin is ≤500 ng/mL and transferrin saturation is ≤30%.":["u59"],"copy|CHAPTER 83 / HAZZARD":["u76"],"kidney-diseases|Kidney Diseases":["u271"],"chronic-kidney-disease|Chronic kidney disease":["u272"],"acute-kidney-injury|Acute kidney injury":["u273"],"localizing-acute-kidney-injury-and-deciding-when-dialysis-is-urgent|Localizing acute kidney injury and deciding when dialysis is urgent":["u134"],"glomerular-and-diabetic-disease|Glomerular and diabetic disease":["u274"],"interstitial-and-renovascular-disease|Interstitial and renovascular disease":["u275"],"dialysis-complications-and-goals-of-care|Dialysis, complications and goals of care":["u276"],"itch-mineral-disease-and-nutrition-during-dialysis|Itch, mineral disease and nutrition during dialysis":["u136"],"starting-trialing-or-withdrawing-dialysis|Starting, trialing or withdrawing dialysis":["u137"],"choosing-a-modality-and-managing-complications|Choosing a modality and managing complications":["u140"],"calcium-oxalate-arthritis-and-red-cell-morphology-in-kidney-failure|Calcium oxalate arthritis and red-cell morphology in kidney failure":["u141"],"key-numbers|Key numbers":["u277"],"israeli-context|Israeli context":["u142"],"test-yourself|Test yourself":["u278"],"copy|Distinguish a new kidney injury from chronic loss of filtration.":["u279"],"copy|Urgency depends on the complication, while mechanism and goals guide longer-term treatment.":["u280"],"copy|A single abnormal creatinine cannot establish chronicity.":["u207"],"copy|Compare previous values and look for albuminuria, blood or casts on urine testing and structural changes on imaging persisting for more than three months.":["u208"],"copy|Albuminuria and reduced filtration each convey prognostic information.":["u209"],"copy|In the US estimate quoted, 47% of adults over 70 met CKD criteria, compared with about 11.5% in all adults.":["u210"],"copy|Most were identified by filtration or albuminuria, long before symptoms of kidney failure (p1277–1279).":["u211"],"copy|CKD criteria: eGFR <60 mL/min/1.73 m² (normal young-adult GFR ~125), albuminuria (ACR >30 mg/g), hematuria (any degree), urinary casts, or imaging abnormality — any persisting >3 months (p1278).":["u281"],"copy|Review blood pressure, cardiovascular risk, diabetes control, medication doses, nephrotoxic exposures and episodes of AKI.":["u212"],"copy|In proteinuric disease, a single ACE inhibitor or ARB can lower pressure and protein loss.":["u213"],"copy|Combining them increases complications.":["u214"],"copy|In diabetes, consider kidney-protective therapy alongside glucose control.":["u215"],"copy|Weigh dietary protein restriction against frailty and unintentional weight loss.":["u216"],"copy|If phosphate remains elevated despite dietary changes, use a binder with meals.":["u217"],"copy|Correct low vitamin D when present and assess persistent secondary hyperparathyroidism separately.":["u218"],"copy|Progressive decline, significant albuminuria, red-cell casts, persistent potassium abnormalities or difficult hypertension are reasons for earlier nephrology involvement and planning (p1279–1280, 1286, 1290).":["u219"],"copy|Nephrology referral triggers (KCP list, p1278): AKI, red cell casts, refractory HTN, K⁺ abnormalities, recurrent/extensive stones, hereditary kidney disease, CKD stage 4–5, severely increased albuminuria.":["u282"],"copy|K/DIGO's own restated list (p1290) phrases the GFR trigger as GFR <30 instead.":["u283"],"copy|An older adult who has taken a diuretic during several days of diarrhea may have a filtration problem before there is structural kidney injury.":["u143"],"copy|Ask separately how much fluid the patient has lost and how much blood is actually reaching the kidneys.":["u144"],"copy|Severe liver disease can redistribute fluid and lower effective circulating volume.":["u145"],"copy|Low cardiac output can do the same even in someone with leg edema and wet lungs.":["u146"],"copy|These are the reasons the cirrhosis vignette points to a prerenal mechanism (p1281–1282).":["u147"],"copy|For an intrinsic lesion, look for evidence of damage within the parenchyma.":["u148"],"copy|Hemolytic–uremic syndrome injures small vessels, prolonged seizures can release myoglobin that injures tubules, and pyelonephritis involves the renal interstitium.":["u149"],"copy|For postrenal AKI, look for a distended bladder, prostatic obstruction or severe fecal impaction.":["u150"],"copy|Unilateral obstruction seldom markedly elevates creatinine when the other kidney works normally.":["u151"],"copy|Both acute heart failure from malignant hypertension and HUS receive credit.":["u284"],"copy|Table 83-1 actually lists the former under prerenal cardiac failure and HUS under intrinsic injury (p1281–1282).":["u153"],"copy|Check the previous creatinine, course of urine output, medications, urine sediment and the possibility of obstruction.":["u154"],"copy|NSAIDs reduce the kidney's ability to maintain inflow during volume depletion.":["u155"],"copy|ACE inhibitors and ARBs reduce pressure on the outflow side of the glomerulus.":["u156"],"copy|With poor intake, an SGLT2 inhibitor's osmotic diuresis may add to fluid loss despite its long-term kidney benefits.":["u157"],"copy|Escitalopram, more characteristically raises concern for hyponatremia (p1279–1283 and empagliflozin and escitalopram prescribing information).":["u285"],"copy|Give isotonic fluid for a demonstrable volume deficit and reassess respiration, circulation and urine output.":["u159"],"copy|Drain bladder outlet obstruction with a catheter.":["u160"],"copy|In an acutely deteriorating patient, pressure improved after fluids, but oliguria persisted, oxygen saturation fell to 88% and pulmonary edema appeared.":["u286"],"copy|The worsening complication was fluid overload.":["u162"],"copy|Potassium remained dangerously high and required treatment even though its trajectory and the acidosis had improved.":["u163"],"copy|Life-threatening, medically refractory fluid, potassium or acid–base abnormalities and symptomatic uremia are reasons for urgent dialysis.":["u164"],"copy|Creatinine alone cannot make that decision (p1283).":["u165"],"copy|Think of heavy proteinuria when an older patient's edema is accompanied by low albumin.":["u166"],"copy|Urinalysis can redirect an assessment initially focused on heart failure.":["u167"],"copy|In the chapter, nephrotic syndrome includes more than 3 g of urinary protein per day, low albumin, edema and dyslipidemia.":["u168"],"copy|Renal vein thrombosis can be a presenting complication.":["u169"],"copy|Measure renal function and protein loss and consider nephrology assessment for the underlying glomerular disorder (p1285).":["u170"],"copy|Diabetic nephropathy is the leading cause of nephrotic syndrome in the general US adult population.":["u287"],"copy|In older adults who actually undergo biopsy for isolated nephrotic syndrome, membranous disease accounts for about 30–40% and minimal change disease for about 15%.":["u172"],"copy|Typical diabetic nephropathy is often diagnosed without biopsy.":["u173"],"copy|A rapid fall in filtration, unusually early heavy proteinuria or absence of expected diabetic microvascular disease should prompt consideration of another disorder.":["u174"],"copy|This selection explains why the most common clinical cause and most common biopsy finding differ (p1285–1286).":["u175"],"copy|Hematuria and proteinuria with rapidly rising creatinine should raise concern for rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis.":["u220"],"copy|Ask about recent infection, systemic symptoms, rash and hemoptysis.":["u221"],"copy|In older adults, postinfectious GN and small-vessel vasculitis can present with nonspecific fatigue or malaise.":["u222"],"copy|Urgent nephrology assessment can guide serology and biopsy.":["u223"],"copy|Frailty, bleeding tendency and small echogenic kidneys affect the risk and likely benefit of biopsy.":["u224"],"copy|Treatment depends on the underlying lesion, the urgency of preserving kidney function and the risks of immunosuppression (p1283–1285).":["u225"],"copy|Drug-induced acute interstitial nephritis may follow a new antibiotic, proton pump inhibitor, NSAID or diuretic.":["u226"],"copy|A thorough exposure history matters when the presentation is simply a rise in creatinine.":["u227"],"copy|Withdraw the suspected drug promptly and follow recovery.":["u228"],"copy|For persistent or uncertain cases, biopsy and corticosteroid treatment require judgment about diagnostic certainty, severity and harms (p1287).":["u229"],"copy|Suspect renovascular disease when hypertension is difficult to control or renal function deteriorates unexpectedly.":["u230"],"copy|Duplex ultrasound can identify a stenosis, but finding an anatomic lesion does not prove it determines the patient's pressure.":["u231"],"copy|A renal vein renin ratio of at least 1.5 favors a pressure response to intervention, with substantial uncertainty for an individual.":["u232"],"copy|Medical therapy is first-line for stable disease.":["u233"],"copy|CORAL found no overall benefit from adding revascularization.":["u234"],"copy|Rapid deterioration with bilateral disease or a solitary functioning kidney warrants a different assessment from stable unilateral narrowing (p1287–1289).":["u235"],"copy|Atherosclerosis accounts for almost 90% of renovascular disease in the geriatric population.":["u288"],"copy|Hazzard calls the other cause “fibrous dysplasia” in the prose (p1287) and Table 83-3 (p1288).":["u289"],"copy|Current practice: Fibromuscular dysplasia. the accepted vascular diagnosis is fibromuscular dysplasia — International consensus, 2019.":["u290"],"copy|An older patient may scratch through the night despite regular hemodialysis.":["u176"],"copy|Examine for dry skin or a drug eruption, review dialysis adequacy and phosphate control, correct iron deficiency when present, and protect and moisturize the skin.":["u177"],"copy|Low-dose gabapentin can help persistent uremic itch.":["u178"],"copy|Reassess for sedation and impaired balance in someone already at risk of falling.":["u179"],"copy|Sedating anticholinergic antihistamines add cognitive and functional burden.":["u180"],"copy|Reducing dialysis frequency or increasing an analgesic opioid does not address the renal mechanism (p1296).":["u181"],"copy|Phosphate retention and reduced production of active vitamin D stimulate PTH release.":["u182"],"copy|Older dialysis patients experience more bone pain and pathological fractures, and older women may have less bone mineral than younger women with similar dialysis duration.":["u183"],"copy|These observations do not imply greater hyperphosphatemia or more severe renal osteodystrophy at older ages.":["u184"],"copy|Active vitamin D concentrations showed no correlation with age in the group described.":["u185"],"copy|Osteoporosis can coexist with renal osteodystrophy.":["u186"],"copy|A history of falls alone does not identify the bone disorder or establish an indication for alendronate (p1295).":["u187"],"copy|For new loss of function, ask who buys food, who prepares it, whether the patient can eat it, and whether nausea followed a change in dialysis.":["u188"],"copy|A list of sodium, phosphate, potassium and fluid restrictions may leave very little the person will actually eat.":["u189"],"copy|Follow weight trends, dietary intake and albumin together.":["u190"],"copy|Low albumin by itself is insufficient to diagnose malnutrition.":["u191"],"copy|A renal dietitian can find acceptable substitutions while protecting energy and protein intake.":["u192"],"copy|Look for weakness, falls, sarcopenia, cognitive change and depression as contributors to declining independence (p1291, 1295–1296).":["u193"],"copy|In a patient with advanced kidney disease, anorexia, fatigue and nausea are familiar manifestations of uremia.":["u194"],"copy|Behavioral change, new confusion, unexplained heart failure or loss of function may be the presenting clue in an older adult.":["u195"],"copy|Establish the previous cognitive and functional baseline and assess other reversible contributors.":["u196"],"copy|A practice case pairs new aggression with an eGFR of 22: stop metformin below 30 mL/min/1.73 m² and evaluate the behavior on its own merits (p1291 and Chapter 99 p1577, Table 99-11).":["u197"],"copy|Kidney failure is defined by a filtration threshold, while the decision to start dialysis also depends on symptoms, difficult-to-control complications and the patient's goals.":["u198"],"copy|When advanced dementia and uremic delirium are hard to distinguish, agree on the purpose and duration of a trial.":["u199"],"copy|The chapter allows 3–4 weeks for uremic cognitive symptoms to clear and describes a planned 4–6 week treatment trial with explicit goals.":["u200"],"copy|Decide how the team will assess comfort, cognition or a hoped-for activity before treatment starts (p1291, 1293–1294).":["u201"],"copy|A cognitively intact patient may ask to stop hemodialysis.":["u291"],"copy|Begin by asking what has become intolerable.":["u203"],"copy|Explore depression, pain, transport burden or loss of independence and assess understanding of the likely consequences.":["u204"],"copy|Document decision-making capacity, involve people the patient wants present and arrange symptom and end-of-life care if he chooses withdrawal.":["u205"],"copy|Frailty, nursing-home residence, albumin and the clinician's estimate of survival can inform this discussion, but they cannot tell the patient how to value treatment time, function or independence (p1296–1297).":["u206"],"copy|1-year dialysis survival for octogenarians/nonagenarians is given as two different numbers in the same chapter: the opening Key Clinical Points box says 50% (p1278), but the \"Prognosis and Survival\" section later states 54% (p1296) for the identical statistic.":["u292"],"copy|Both are printed as-is; know which figure a question is quoting.":["u293"],"copy|Conventional center hemodialysis requires repeated travel and reliable vascular access.":["u236"],"copy|A fistula needs time to mature, while a catheter permits rapid access at the cost of greater bacteremia risk.":["u237"],"copy|Peritoneal dialysis allows home treatment and more control over the schedule, but the patient or caregiver must manage exchanges and recognize complications such as peritonitis.":["u238"],"copy|Nocturnal or cyclic treatment can accommodate rehabilitation in trained facilities.":["u239"],"copy|Assess frailty, function, cognition and infection risk when evaluating transplantation.":["u240"],"copy|Comprehensive conservative care includes active treatment of fluid, electrolyte and anemia problems alongside relief of pain, itch, poor sleep and other symptoms (p1291–1294).":["u241"],"copy|Renal anemia typically reflects inadequate erythropoietin and a hypoproliferative marrow.":["u242"],"copy|Check for iron deficiency and bleeding, especially when indices differ from the usual normocytic pattern.":["u243"],"copy|During erythropoietin treatment, increasing red-cell production can exhaust previously sufficient iron stores.":["u244"],"copy|Cardiovascular mortality remains high on dialysis, so assess for active disease and address conventional vascular risks, anemia and fluid overload in the context of the patient's goals (p1294).":["u245"],"copy|Iron status matters when interpreting the hemoglobin response to erythropoietin.":["u246"],"copy|Anemia in CKD is typically normochromic and normocytic and develops around GFR 35 mL/min.":["u294"],"copy|Hazzard prints a conservative hemoglobin target “near 11.5 mg/dL” (p1294). [sic — hemoglobin is measured in g/dL here.]":["u295"],"copy|Current practice: Hemoglobin target with an ESA. in adults receiving an ESA, target hemoglobin below 11.5 g/dL — KDIGO 2026, recommendation 3.3.1.":["u296"],"copy|Hazzard describes ferritin <500 ng/mL or transferrin saturation <30% under its historical “functional iron deficiency” wording (p1294).":["u297"],"copy|Current practice: Iron treatment during hemodialysis. for anemic patients receiving hemodialysis, KDIGO 2026 recommendation 2.1 suggests starting iron when both ferritin is ≤500 ng/mL and transferrin saturation is ≤30%.":["u298"],"copy|When a dialysis patient develops crystal arthritis, ask about vitamin C supplements.":["u247"],"copy|The body converts ascorbic acid to oxalate, and dialysis clears it incompletely.":["u248"],"copy|Synovial calcium oxalate deposits can therefore provoke arthritis.":["u249"],"copy|Sevelamer and lanthanum bind dietary phosphate and do not supply oxalate.":["u250"],"copy|Related Harrison teaching, ch372 p2867.":["u251"],"copy|Separate marrow production from smear appearance.":["u252"],"copy|Lack of erythropoietin can produce a hypoproliferative anemia.":["u253"],"copy|Uremia can also change the membrane of circulating red cells, producing echinocytes with evenly spaced projections.":["u254"],"copy|Howell–Jolly bodies are nuclear remnants within a cell, while target cells have a central area of hemoglobin.":["u255"],"copy|Related Harrison teaching, ch63 p434, Figure 63-10.":["u256"],"copy|1-year survival for octogenarians/nonagenarians after starting dialysis: 50% per the chapter's own Key Clinical Points (p1278) — the text and table differ.":["u299"],"copy|בקבלת מטופל למכון דיאליזה בקהילה בוחנים גם את הניידות, הסיכון לנפילות, הכאב והתמיכה המשפחתית.":["u257"],"copy|כלי הבקרה של משרד הבריאות בודק אם המטופל ומשפחתו קיבלו הסבר על החלופות, לרבות טיפול פליאטיבי, ואם בוצעה הערכה תזונתית מלאה תוך 45 יום מהקבלה.":["u258"],"copy|משרד הבריאות, כלי בקרה לדיאליזה בקהילה, עדכון 02.06.2026, עמ׳ 16, סעיפים 5.3–5.7":["u259"],"copy|אצל מטופל יציב מבחינה מטבולית שנמצא במכון מעל שלושה חודשים, כלי הבקרה בוחן הערכה תזונתית לפחות אחת לשנה וכן כשיש שינוי במצב, חריגה בבדיקות או בקשה של המטופל או המשפחה.":["u260"],"copy|אצל מטופל לא יציב נבדקת הערכה חודשית או לפי שיקול דעת התזונאי.":["u261"],"copy|בתיעוד בודקים גם יכולת לקנות מזון, לבשל ולאכול, קשיי לעיסה ובליעה וצריכה בפועל.":["u262"],"copy|משרד הבריאות, כלי הבקרה, עמ׳ 17–18, סעיפים 5.18–5.19":["u263"],"copy|בהסבר משרד הבריאות על חוק החולה הנוטה למות מובחנים שני מצבים בסוף החיים.":["u264"],"copy|כאשר מתקיימים תנאי החוק, אפשר להימנע מחידוש טיפול מחזורי שיש לו נקודת סיום ברורה.":["u265"],"copy|אסור להפסיק טיפול רציף אם ההפסקה עלולה להביא למות החולה.":["u266"],"copy|כשאדם יכול להביע את רצונו, מבררים אותו ישירות.":["u267"],"copy|אצל מטופל המבקש לוותר על דיאליזה חשוב לברר גם את סיבת הבקשה ואת הסבל שאפשר להקל, כפי שמתואר בפרק בעמ׳ 1296–1297.":["u268"],"copy|משרד הבריאות, המתת חסד, 25.05.2026, הסעיפים ״מה החוק לגבי המתת חסד בישראל״ ו״מה מותר ואסור לעשות במקרה של מחלה סופנית״":["u269"],"copy|June 2023 Q67 Pulmonary aging and FEV1 p1231":["u300"],"copy|June 2022 Q3 Drug-associated AKI p1279":["u301"],"copy|May 2024 Q22 Intrinsic kidney injury p1281":["u302"],"copy|June 2026 Q88 Prerenal physiology in cirrhosis p1281":["u303"],"copy|May 2024 Q49 Nephrotic syndrome in later life p1285":["u304"],"copy|May 2024 Q81 Oxalate deposition and vitamin C Harrison p2867":["u305"],"copy|June 2023 Basic Q116 Burr cells in uremia Harrison p434":["u306"],"copy|December 2021 Q21 Urgent dialysis for fluid overload p1283":["u307"],"copy|June 2025 Q43 Nutrition during dialysis p1295":["u308"],"copy|June 2026 Q77 Bone disease in older dialysis patients p1295":["u309"],"copy|June 2023 Q39 A request to stop dialysis p1296":["u310"],"copy|May 2024 Q44 Uremic pruritus on dialysis p1296":["u311"],"copy|June 2026 Q33 Gabapentin for uremic itch p1296":["u312"],"copy|May 2024 Q81 Vitamin C and oxalate Harrison p2867":["u305"],"copy|December 2021 Q21 Dialysis for fluid overload p1283":["u307"],"copy|June 2026 Q77 Bone disease on dialysis p1295":["u309"]},"maxId":312,"sequenceOffsets":[[331,-294],[333,-43],[334,-3],[335,-39],[336,-4],[337,-38]]}
